== Introduction
<introduction>
This lesson details the #strong[Sunan of Ṭawāf (سنن الطواف)]
\[recommended acts of ṭawāf\]: the #strong[Istilām (استلام)]
\[greeting/touching\] of #strong[Ḥajar al-Aswad (الحجر الأسود)] \[the
Black Stone\] at the start of every round, #strong[Idtibāʿ (اضطباع)]
\[baring the right shoulder\], #strong[Ramal (رمل)] \[brisk walking\] in
the first three rounds, and the differing rulings on greeting the four
#strong[corners (arkān)] of the Kaʿbah. It then covers the #strong[two
wājib rakʿahs] after ṭawāf (at Maqām Ibrāhīm), and the protocol of
returning to Ḥajar al-Aswad for one more Istilām before beginning
#strong[Saʿī].

#horizontalrule

== Part 1 --- Istilām of Ḥajar al-Aswad in Every Round
<part-1-istilam-of-hajar-al-aswad-in-every-round>
Ṭawāf begins #strong[from Ḥajar al-Aswad] and each round #strong[ends at
Ḥajar al-Aswad] --- so every round begins there. At the start of each
round he performs #strong[Istilām]:

- #strong[If possible without harming anyone:] touch it / kiss it ---
  this was the #strong[ʿādah (practice)] of the Prophet ﷺ.
- #strong[If not possible:] simply #strong[face it], say #strong["Allāhu
  Akbar,"] and #strong[raise the hands toward it] --- like raising the
  hands to begin ṣalāh, but #strong[only up to the shoulders] (not up to
  the ears).

Since #strong[every round is a new round], the Istilām is #strong[masnūn
in every round], just like the first.

#horizontalrule

== Part 2 --- Idtibāʿ (Baring the Right Shoulder)
<part-2-idtiba-baring-the-right-shoulder>
In iḥrām there are two sheets --- one covering the #strong[lower] body,
one covering the #strong[upper] body. For ṭawāf, one performs
#strong[Idtibāʿ] with the upper sheet:

- Pass the upper sheet #strong[under the right armpit/shoulder] (not
  over it), and leave it #strong[over the left shoulder].
- Result: the #strong[right shoulder is exposed], the #strong[left
  shoulder is covered].

#strong[Etymology:] from #strong[ḍabuʿ (ضبع)], meaning the #strong[ʿaḍud
(عضد)] \[upper arm\] --- because it exposes the upper arm/right shoulder
in iḥrām. Hence the name #emph[Idtibāʿ].

#horizontalrule

== Part 3 --- Ramal (Brisk Walking in the First Three Rounds)
<part-3-ramal-brisk-walking-in-the-first-three-rounds>
#strong[Ramal] is a manner of walking: #strong[stretching out the
shoulders] and walking #strong[a bit faster] (with vigour).

- Done in the #strong[first three rounds] of ṭawāf, #strong[when one
  intends to perform Saʿī after the ṭawāf].
- In a #strong[crowd] where one cannot stretch the shoulders, take one's
  time --- and perform it #strong[when space is available].

#horizontalrule

== Part 4 --- Istilām of the Corners (Arkān)
<part-4-istilam-of-the-corners-arkan>
The Kaʿbah has four corners. Their rulings differ:

=== Rukn al-Yamānī (the corner reached just before Ḥajar al-Aswad)
<rukn-al-yamani-the-corner-reached-just-before-hajar-al-aswad>
Three opinions on greeting #strong[Rukn al-Yamānī (الركن اليماني)]:

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (42.86%, 57.14%),
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Imam], [Ruling],),
    table.hline(),
    [#strong[Imam Abū Ḥanīfah]], [#strong[Mustaḥabb] (recommended) ---
    #emph[not] masnūn],
    [#strong[Imam Muḥammad]], [#strong[Masnūn] (sunnah)],
    [#strong[Imam al-Shāfiʿī]], [#strong[Istilām + Takbīr + Taqbīl
    (kissing)] --- all masnūn],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

- In the #strong[Ḥanafī] school, only the #strong[Istilām] (not kissing)
  is at issue.
- #strong[Evidence for it being sunnah (Imam Muḥammad):] it is narrated
  (from Ibn ʿUmar and others) that the Prophet ﷺ performed Istilām of
  #strong[these two] --- Ḥajar al-Aswad and Rukn al-Yamānī --- and not
  the other two corners. (Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported the Prophet
  ﷺ would even place his cheek upon it.)
- #strong[Evidence for it being only mustaḥabb (the other Ḥanafī view):]
  there is a difference in #strong[daraja (degree)] ---
  #strong[mustaḥabb is lower], #strong[sunnah is higher]. Had it been a
  full sunnah like Ḥajar al-Aswad, the #strong[kissing (taqbīl)] would
  have been attached to it too. Since it is not, the Istilām of Rukn
  al-Yamānī is a #strong[step below] that of Ḥajar al-Aswad --- hence
  #strong[mustaḥabb].

=== Rukn al-ʿIrāqī and Rukn al-Shāmī (the other two corners)
<rukn-al-iraqi-and-rukn-al-shami-the-other-two-corners>
These come #strong[after] Ḥajar al-Aswad. #strong[No Istilām] is done
for them --- the view of the #strong[majority of the Ṣaḥābah], adopted
by the #strong[Aḥnāf]. (A minority of Ṣaḥābah held all four corners
recommended.)

#strong[Why the majority view is stronger --- two arguments:]

+ #strong[No basis in the Sunnah:] greeting these two did #strong[not]
  come from the Prophet ﷺ; it was the practice of #strong[some Ṣaḥābah].
  An ʿibādah is established on the basis of #strong[naṣṣ / the Prophet's
  ﷺ practice], which is absent here.
+ #strong[They are not true corners:] the word #strong[rukn (ركن)]
  literally means "corner." But these two are #strong[not on the actual
  foundation] of the Baytullāh --- because the #strong[Ḥaṭīm / Ḥijr
  (الحطيم / الحِجر)] on that side is #strong[part of the Baytullāh] yet
  was #strong[not included in the (later) construction]. So these two
  are really the #strong[middle of the wall], not genuine corners.
  Istilām, by contrast, is for Ḥajar al-Aswad and for a true corner
  (Rukn al-Yamānī).

#quote(block: true)[
#strong[Consequence:] because the Ḥaṭīm is part of the Baytullāh, it is
#strong[compulsory to make ṭawāf around the Ḥaṭīm as well] --- one may
#strong[not] pass #strong[between] the wall of the Baytullāh (on that
side) and the Ḥaṭīm.
]

#horizontalrule

== Part 5 --- The Two Rakʿahs of Ṭawāf
<part-5-the-two-rakahs-of-awaf>
After completing all #strong[seven rounds], he prays #strong[two
rakʿahs]:

- #strong[Better] performed near #strong[Maqām Ibrāhīm (مقام إبراهيم)]
  if easily possible; otherwise #strong[anywhere in Masjid al-Ḥarām].
- These rakʿahs are #strong[wājib].

=== Wājib vs.~Sunnah (Ḥanafī vs.~Shāfiʿī)
<wajib-vs-sunnah-hanafi-vs-shafii>
- #strong[Imam al-Shāfiʿī:] since the Shāfiʿī school has #strong[no
  "wājib" category] (a thing is either #strong[farḍ] or
  #strong[sunnah]), and these are not farḍ, they fall under
  #strong[sunnah] (the Prophet ﷺ performed them consistently).
- #strong[Ḥanafī:] three categories --- #strong[farḍ] (obligation from a
  #strong[definitive/qaṭʿī] dalīl), #strong[wājib] (obligation from a
  #strong[probable/ẓannī] dalīl), and #strong[sunnah]. These two rakʿahs
  are #strong[wājib].

=== Evidence
<evidence>
#strong[Qur'ān:]

#quote(block: true)[
وَاتَّخِذُوا مِن مَّقَامِ إِبْرَاهِيمَ مُصَلًّى

#emph["And take the Station of Ibrāhīm as a place of prayer."]

#strong[Ref:] al-Baqarah: 125
]

- This is a #strong[command], and a command minimally demands
  #strong[wujūb]. But the dalīl is #strong[not perfectly explicit] about
  #emph[these two rakʿahs specifically], so it cannot make them
  #strong[farḍ] --- hence #strong[wājib].

#strong[About Maqām Ibrāhīm:] it is the #strong[stone bearing the marks
of Ibrāhīm's ﷺ feet], upon which he used to stand (when
dismounting/mounting his animal) while visiting Hājar and Ismāʿīl عليهما
السلام, whom he had left in Makkah by Allah's command. #strong[ʿUmar رضي
الله عنه] expressed the wish that it be taken as a place of prayer ---
and the āyah was later revealed accordingly (one of the #emph[muwāfaqāt]
of ʿUmar).

#strong[Ḥadīth:] when the Prophet ﷺ finished ṭawāf, he came to
#strong[Maqām Ibrāhīm], prayed #strong[two rakʿahs], and #strong[recited
this āyah] as the reference for his act --- showing the āyah refers to
the two rakʿahs of ṭawāf #strong[\(hadith as cited in class --- confirm
in hadith index)].

#strong[Practice of ʿUmar رضي الله عنه:] he once #strong[forgot] the two
rakʿahs of ṭawāf and later performed them at a place called #strong[Dhū
Ṭuwā] --- indicating they are #strong[wājib] (otherwise there would be
no need to make them up).

#horizontalrule

== Part 6 --- Returning to Ḥajar al-Aswad Before Saʿī
<part-6-returning-to-hajar-al-aswad-before-sai>
After the two rakʿahs --- and #strong[before beginning Saʿī] --- he
returns to #strong[Ḥajar al-Aswad] and performs #strong[Istilām once
more]. So the #strong[start of Saʿī mirrors the start of ṭawāf].

#strong[Ruling:] wherever a #strong[Saʿī follows the ṭawāf] (as here,
with the intention of Saʿī), the protocol is: go to Ḥajar al-Aswad →
Istilām → then Saʿī. If there is #strong[no intention of Saʿī] after
this (optional) ṭawāf, there is #strong[no need] to return to Ḥajar
al-Aswad.

- #strong[Another (non-adopted) view:] reported that he does
  #strong[not] return to Ḥajar al-Aswad --- he goes to Saʿī directly.
  #strong[This is not taken in the Ḥanafī school.]
- #strong[Ḥanafī dalīl:] the Prophet ﷺ #strong[returned and performed
  Istilām] of Ḥajar al-Aswad. Because #strong[Saʿī is dependent on the
  ṭawāf], it should be #strong[begun the same way ṭawāf is begun] ---
  with Istilām.

=== The gap (faṣl) between ṭawāf and Saʿī
<the-gap-fasl-between-awaf-and-sai>
It is #strong[makrūh] to make a #strong[faṣl (فصل)] \[gap\] between
ṭawāf and Saʿī. #strong[Question:] doesn't going back for the Istilām
create a gap? #strong[Answer:] this Istilām is #strong[counted as part
of the ṭawāf], not a gap --- so it does not break the connection. (This
applies specifically when he is doing #strong[Saʿī after the ṭawāf]\.)

#horizontalrule

== Part 7 --- Beginning Saʿī (Preview)
<part-7-beginning-sai-preview>
After the Istilām, he proceeds to #strong[al-Ṣafā] and begins
#strong[Saʿī from Ṣafā to Marwah] --- the order in which the Qur'ān
mentions them:

#quote(block: true)[
إِنَّ الصَّفَا وَالْمَرْوَةَ مِن شَعَائِرِ اللَّهِ

#emph["Indeed, al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah are among the symbols of Allah."]

#strong[Ref:] al-Baqarah: 158
]

#strong[Which gate to exit from?] It is #strong[not specified]. A
narration reports the Prophet ﷺ exited via #strong[Bāb al-Ṣafā], but per
the Ḥanafī reading this was #strong[coincidence] (it was simply closest
to Ṣafā) --- #strong[not masnūn]. One may exit from #strong[any gate].

#horizontalrule

== Closing
<closing>
- #strong[Covered:] Istilām of Ḥajar al-Aswad each round (touch/kiss if
  possible, else face it, takbīr, raise hands to the shoulders);
  #strong[Idtibāʿ] (baring the right shoulder); #strong[Ramal] in the
  first three rounds when Saʿī will follow; the corners --- #strong[Rukn
  al-Yamānī] (mustaḥabb per Abū Ḥanīfah / masnūn per Muḥammad / with
  kissing per Shāfiʿī) and #strong[no Istilām] for Rukn al-ʿIrāqī &
  al-Shāmī (no Sunnah basis; not true corners because of the Ḥaṭīm ---
  hence ṭawāf must include the Ḥaṭīm); the #strong[two wājib rakʿahs] at
  Maqām Ibrāhīm (āyah + ḥadīth + ʿUmar's practice); returning to Ḥajar
  al-Aswad for Istilām before Saʿī (counted as part of ṭawāf, not a
  makrūh gap); and beginning Saʿī from Ṣafā.
- #strong[Next class:] #strong[how to perform Saʿī] --- what is done at
  Ṣafā and Marwah, and its details; same time inshā'Allāh.
